{"key":"et0","updated":"2026-11-02","label":"Evaporative demand","title":"Evaporative demand (ET₀) compared with normal","subtitle":"Aug–Oct 2026, against the same months in 1991–2020","source":"gridMET reference evapotranspiration (grass), averaged by parish","demo":false,"build":1,"categories":[{"key":"very_low","label":"Much lower demand","note":"lowest 10% of years","color":"#2166ac"},{"key":"low","label":"Lower demand","note":"lowest 10 to 30% of years","color":"#92c5de"},{"key":"normal","label":"Near normal","note":"middle 40% of years","color":"#f3efe4"},{"key":"high","label":"Higher demand","note":"highest 10 to 30% of years","color":"#e08214"},{"key":"very_high","label":"Much higher demand","note":"highest 10% of years","color":"#8c510a"},{"key":"not_rated","label":"Not rated","note":"too few years or days missing","color":"#bdbdbd"}],"rank":{"high":"Highest demand compared with normal","low":"Lowest demand compared with normal","note":"Ranked by evaporative demand as a percentage of normal."},"how":"Reference evapotranspiration (ET₀) is the water a well-watered grass surface would lose to the air in a day, worked out from temperature, sunshine, humidity and wind. This shows the total for the last three complete months (Aug–Oct 2026) compared with the same months in 1991 to 2020. A high number means the air is pulling more water out of soil and plants than usual, so the soil dries faster for the same rain.","limits":["ET₀ is demand for a reference grass surface. It is not the water a particular crop or field uses.","It comes from gridMET, a 4 km grid that mixes PRISM with a land model, using modelled sunshine, humidity and wind. It is an estimate.","A parish value is the average of the grid cells inside it.","High demand is not drought by itself. Read it together with rainfall and soil moisture."],"credits":[{"text":"Evaporative demand: gridMET, Abatzoglou, J.T. (2013), Int. J. Climatology 33: 121-131","url":"https://www.climatologylab.org/gridmet.html"},{"